// run
// #5348: TWO `jsx_text` runs in a row, which is what a `<` that starts no tag
// produces. `atJsxStart` is false for `<` followed by a number (or by `(`, or
// by nothing), so `parseJsxChildren` falls through to the text branch, which
// absorbs that `<` as one byte of literal text and scans on — leaving the
// turn after it another text run.
//
// That second run hung the compiler. `parseJsxText` re-derived its START from
// `p.toks[p.pos - 1].end`, but the first run's `jsxResync` rebuilds the token
// tail and deliberately leaves `p.pos - 1` naming the token BEFORE the whole
// run — the opening tag's `>`. So run two began where run one began, ended
// where run one ended, and the loop turned for ever: `bit check` never
// returned on `<p>a < 3</p>`, which is ordinary prose, and the fuzzer found
// it as a hang (_tests_/fuzz/crashes/hang-414710f0571de5ec.bit). Released
// 0.17.0 runs this program, so terminating on it is released behaviour
// restored, not a widening — the `<` has always been text here.
//
// The SPACING is #5333's, not 0.17.0's, and writing it out is half the point
// of the `.expected`: 0.17.0 prints `a  &lt; 3` with TWO spaces, because it
// rebuilt text from tokens and tokens carry no spacing. One space is what the
// source says, which is what SPEC §12.12's raw run means.
//
// A text run can also end on a `<` the token cursor never reaches at all —
// one the eager whole-file lex swallowed inside comment bytes. That face is
// in `_tests_/bit/jsxconformance.bit` rather than here, because `bit fmt`
// re-emits those bytes as a real trailing comment and no fixture on disk can
// hold them.
import { Node, render, elem, attr, frag, text } from "../../pkg/web"

fn main() {
  let spaced = <p>a < 3 and b <4</p>
  println(render(spaced))
}
